```   The code implements a greedy strategy that always prefers the letter with the higher remaining count, but switches to the other letter if appending the preferred letter would create three in a row. This ensures we never exceed two consecutive identical letters while respecting the counts. The approach works because the problem guarantees a valid string exists, so we never run out of the alternative letter when needed. For example, with a=4 and b=1, it produces "aabaa" by placing 'b' exactly when two 'a's are already present. Similarly, for a=1 and b=2, it yields "bba". The loop continues until both counts reach zero, and the join converts the list back to a string. This is efficient (O(a+b) time) and handles all edge cases like one count being zero (up to two of the other letter).

